body:
I have found out the cause:

``` ts
// Does not work IN SAFARI
date: string = '2016-01-20T19:00:00+0000';

// Works IN SAFARI
date: string = '2016-01-20T19:00:00';
```

Both work in Google Chrome of course.

Here is the line in the `angular` source:

https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/master/modules/%40angular/common/src/pipes/date_pipe.ts#L109

This will work as a temporary solution. Of course, you can replace the replaced string value with what you require, e.g. hyphen instead of comma. 
```    // Used due to issue for Safari in formatting date. 
    formatDate(obj) {
        return obj.toString().replace(/,/g, "/");
    }
```
Then use it like this: 

```
{{ formatDate(myDateObject) }}
```
